  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nBuilding surface/upper ridge over much of the eastern CONUS will\nbring increasing temperatures and humidity through midweek.\nNBM probs of 95F/100F continue to increase across the area\nTuesday into Wednesday, with Wednesday looking like the hottest\nday of the week. With a lingering frontal boundary just north of\nthe area through the first half of the week, some uncertainty\nremains with heat potential as upstream convection and cloud\ncover could limit heating during this period. However,\nconfidence is increasing for heat headline needed for at least a\nportion of the area midweek. \n \nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nContinuing to monitor convective potential through midweek with\nactive zonal jet pattern through the Great Lakes and the local\narea on the periphery of the ridge with building instability.\nModels are hinting at a few windows of convective potential for\nthe local area. Aside front isentropic ascent/low level jet \nforced convection upstream across northern IN/southwest lower MI\nearly this morning that could sneak into northwest OH, next \nbest chance of impactful shower/storm activity across the area \nis late tonight. Low level jet refocuses isentropic ascent just \nnorth of the forecast area, with training convection possibly \nsneaking into northwest PA late tonight into Monday morning. \nSome higher PMM QPF signals in the HREF/REFS are enough to \nconsider at least a small chance of heavy rainfall somewhere in \nthe northwest PA/western NY area, with WPC highlighting the area\nin a Day 1 marginal risk for excessive rainfall. This could \nresult in very isolated flash flooding somewhere in the region, \nbut confidence is too low on coverage and placement at the \nmoment to warrant headlines or amplified messaging.\n\nDifficult to pinpoint convective potential beyond the next 24\nhours or so given the pattern, but with quasi-zonal/northwest\nflow through the Great Lakes into midweek and building\ninstability, there could be some organized convective complexes\nthat impact the area in the Monday night through Tuesday night\ntimeframe. Ridging becomes more prominent across the local area\nWednesday so would expect this day to be the least active\nconvection-wise, but chances increase across the area late\nWednesday/Thursday as a cold front sinks into the area.",
